// One turn of the loop: prompt, read, run.
//
// The prompt is written to the console only. The serial port gets the prompt
// and the line together, once the line is known, because `print` is the only
// way to the serial port and it always ends a line -- so a prompt sent there
// on its own would put the command a person typed on the row below it and the
// transcript a test reads would not look like a session.
sh_once() {
  string t = sh_prompt();
  con_str(t);
  sh_go(t, sh_line());
} return void;

// The echo of one command to the serial port, and then the command.
sh_go(string t, string s) {
  print(t + s);
  sh_exec(s);
} return void;

// The prompt carries the working directory, which is the only thing on screen
// that says `cd` did anything.
sh_prompt() {
} return string fs_pwd(fs_here()) + " $ ";
